WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:02.200
- Thanks to Indochino
for keeping Legal Eagle

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:04.334
in the air and helping me look fly.

00:00:04.334 --> 00:00:05.270
(eagle screeches)

00:00:05.270 --> 00:00:07.800
- They can have visions of the future

00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:12.610
or see the dead and sometimes
be uniquely susceptible

00:00:12.610 --> 00:00:15.540
to invasion by an entity
that is alien to them.

00:00:15.540 --> 00:00:16.373
- Objection.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:18.950
- Finally, geez.

00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:19.952
- [Judge] On what grounds?

00:00:19.952 --> 00:00:20.785
(hands slamming on table)

00:00:20.785 --> 00:00:22.000
- How about silliness, your Honor?

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:24.580
- No, silliness is not a valid objection.

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:27.670
Object to relevance or
object to improper opinion.

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:29.885
Silliness is not in the
Federal Rules of Evidence.

00:00:29.885 --> 00:00:33.610
(bright upbeat music)

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:36.170
Hey, Legal Eagles, it's
time to think like a lawyer.

00:00:36.170 --> 00:00:38.780
And today, in a special Halloween episode,

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:41.270
we are covering The
Exorcism of Emily Rose.

00:00:41.270 --> 00:00:43.860
Now I'd like to apologize ahead of time.

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:46.930
We're having some technical
difficulties here in my office,

00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:49.060
and I didn't get a whole
lot of sleep last night.

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:50.650
I woke up at three o'clock in the morning

00:00:50.650 --> 00:00:51.922
and couldn't get back to sleep, and--

00:00:51.922 --> 00:00:54.190
(slow creepy music)
(girls laughing)

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:55.690
- [Man] Indochino.

00:00:57.960 --> 00:01:01.860
- Yeah, so anyway, be sure
to comment in the form

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:05.370
of an objection, which I will
either sustain or overrule,

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:07.580
and stick around until
the end of the video

00:01:07.580 --> 00:01:10.560
where I give The Exorcism
of Emily Rose a grade

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:11.940
for legal realism.

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:13.040
So
(lights buzzing)

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:15.440
(woman cackling evilly)

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:18.120
I guess without further ado,

00:01:18.120 --> 00:01:22.467
let's dig into The Exorcism of Emily Rose.

00:01:22.467 --> 00:01:25.650
(bright jaunty music)

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:28.020
So apparently, this movie is
based on a real-life story

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:30.580
where a woman who suffered
from severe epilepsy

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:32.390
had her family call in a priest

00:01:32.390 --> 00:01:34.650
who administered an
exorcism on her believing

00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:38.310
that she was possessed by
demons or ghosts or something.

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:40.100
And during the course of the exorcism,

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:43.500
she died potentially of
neglect, potentially of abuse.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:45.020
The priest was then put on trial,

00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:48.520
and this trial is a mishmash
of the things happening

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:51.730
at the criminal trial
for negligent homicide

00:01:51.730 --> 00:01:54.740
as well as what may or may
not have happened depending

00:01:54.740 --> 00:01:57.000
on who you talk to during the course

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:58.670
of this supposed exorcism.

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:00.723
- In the case before you,
the evidence will show

00:02:00.723 --> 00:02:04.040
that the victim had a
serious medical condition.

00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:05.020
- Oh, wow.
- that demanded

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:06.300
medical treatment.

00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:08.600
We will prove that the victim's condition

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:13.070
rendered her physically and
psychologically incapable

00:02:13.070 --> 00:02:14.413
of caring for herself.

00:02:15.290 --> 00:02:17.670
- Wow, so there's a lot of stuff going on

00:02:17.670 --> 00:02:19.190
in the prosecution's opening statement

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:23.110
that is just, it's perfectly realistic

00:02:23.110 --> 00:02:26.270
in a way that you rarely ever
see in movies and television.

00:02:26.270 --> 00:02:28.770
First, the prosecutor is
standing at the lectern,

00:02:28.770 --> 00:02:31.380
which was set up specifically
for the arguments to the jury.

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:32.490
He's using notes.

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:35.850
Often, we get this idea that lawyers

00:02:35.850 --> 00:02:37.670
should be talking extemporaneously

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:40.240
and yelling in front of the jury

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:42.500
when, really, it's more
likely to be like this.

00:02:42.500 --> 00:02:44.210
He's just telling the facts.

00:02:44.210 --> 00:02:47.010
And, in fact, the specific
wording that he used

00:02:47.010 --> 00:02:50.100
is "the facts will show"
or "we will prove."

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.010
An opening statement is not argument.

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:55.350
You're just showing the jury
or you're telling the jury

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:58.320
what you think the facts are
going to show in the future.

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:00.950
And the tone that he's
using is right, too.

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:02.070
This is a somber occasion.

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:03.670
This is not the time for yelling.

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:05.990
You're not going to put
yourself out on a limb.

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:08.420
You're just gonna talk about the dry facts

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:11.180
that are going to be
elicited in a persuasive way.

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:14.540
So this actually might be
the most realistic portrayal

00:03:14.540 --> 00:03:16.510
of a district attorney's
opening statement I

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:19.283
have ever seen in a movie; it's amazing.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:22.700
- This is what she looked like

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:29.143
before the defendant began
his religious treatment.

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:35.774
This is a photograph taken of
her on the day that she died.

00:03:35.774 --> 00:03:36.640
(jurors gasp)

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:39.180
- This is also pretty realistic as well.

00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:43.000
There's no reason to pound
the table and yell and scream

00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:46.520
when you have just a very,
very powerful visual image.

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:49.540
And so a real-life prosecutor
would absolutely want

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:53.100
to use the strength of
that particular evidence

00:03:53.100 --> 00:03:55.971
without trying to beat the
jury over the head with it.

00:03:55.971 --> 00:03:58.150
- [Dr. Vogel] I've known
Emily her entire life.

00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:00.920
I took care of all of the Rose girls.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:03.870
- [Ethan] Can you describe Emily
as you knew her growing up?

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:06.940
- Okay, so this is, I
guess, a family physician.

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:08.680
This could be a really tricky witness

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:11.480
because, on the one hand,
they're a medical professional.

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:14.210
But on the other hand,
they're a lay witness.

00:04:14.210 --> 00:04:17.840
They've seen the, in
this case, the victim,

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:19.810
so they have firsthand knowledge.

00:04:19.810 --> 00:04:22.650
And it's a question of whether
you're calling this person

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:25.130
as an expert witness
or are you calling them

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:26.830
as a percipient lay witness.

00:04:26.830 --> 00:04:28.680
You can get into a little bit of trouble

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:30.420
when you go past the percipient witness

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:31.960
into asking them to opine based

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:33.390
on their medical testimony.

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:34.950
Let's see what happens here.

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:37.500
- She was a bit sickly in her early years.

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:41.843
She stayed inside a lot,
reading, learning music.

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:44.130
- How did she feel about
going away to school?

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:45.310
- Objection, speculation.

00:04:45.310 --> 00:04:47.030
The witness is not a psychiatrist.

00:04:47.030 --> 00:04:50.360
- Well, even if the
witness was a psychiatrist,

00:04:50.360 --> 00:04:53.640
there's no way that she can
know the internal mental state

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:54.610
of the victim.

00:04:54.610 --> 00:04:56.550
You can find out about what they said

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:59.050
or how they manifested themselves,

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:01.580
which might lead to an
inference of what they felt.

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:03.310
But you can't ask the actual question

00:05:03.310 --> 00:05:05.650
how did someone feel or
what did someone think?

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:06.846
That's totally improper.

00:05:06.846 --> 00:05:10.300
(Emily screaming and crying)

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:11.460
- [Ethan] Dr. Mueller,
what's your position

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:12.800
at the university hospital?

00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:15.610
- I am chairman of the
Department of Neurology.

00:05:15.610 --> 00:05:18.220
- And after you heard about
the dorm room incident,

00:05:18.220 --> 00:05:19.310
what were your initial thoughts?

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:20.143
- Oh, boy.

00:05:20.143 --> 00:05:23.170
So for an expert witness,
especially a doctor,

00:05:23.170 --> 00:05:26.200
you would spend a really,
really long time building

00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:28.050
up their bona fides and their credibility

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:31.620
and their past CV, their
accolades, their education.

00:05:31.620 --> 00:05:33.410
You wouldn't just say what's your position

00:05:33.410 --> 00:05:36.050
at this hospital and then dig
into their expert opinion.

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:37.700
You've gotta lay the foundation that,

00:05:37.700 --> 00:05:39.930
number one, this person is
qualified to give testimony

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:42.010
in court, so you satisfy the judge.

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:45.160
But also, you want the jury to
actually believe this person.

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:47.560
So you wanna build them up
so they believe your expert

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:49.350
instead of the defense's expert.

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:53.630
So both for legal reasons
and for persuasive reasons,

00:05:53.630 --> 00:05:55.260
you really have to spend
a lot of time building

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:57.420
up your experts, not like this.

00:05:57.420 --> 00:05:59.920
- So suspecting that Emily was epileptic,

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:00.910
how did you proceed?

00:06:00.910 --> 00:06:03.000
- I administered an electroencephalograph.

00:06:03.000 --> 00:06:04.660
- [Ethan] What did the EEG reveal?

00:06:04.660 --> 00:06:06.960
- It showed a possible epileptic focus

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:09.230
in the patient's left temporal lobe.

00:06:09.230 --> 00:06:11.170
- [Ethan] Did this require
a specific treatment?

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:12.730
- Yes, I began a medication schedule

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:14.780
for Emily with the drug Gambutrol.

00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:17.410
I also advised regular
followup examinations

00:06:17.410 --> 00:06:19.930
and further tests, but Emily failed

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:22.578
to keep her followup appointments with me.

00:06:22.578 --> 00:06:23.511
- Did she give any reason

00:06:23.511 --> 00:06:25.320
for discontinuing these appointments?

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:26.613
- Yes, she told me she was convinced

00:06:26.613 --> 00:06:29.310
that her condition was a spiritual one.

00:06:29.310 --> 00:06:31.850
- Okay, here you have some
really interesting testimony

00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:34.460
that I think most people
would misconstrue.

00:06:34.460 --> 00:06:36.040
Most people hear an
out-of-court statement.

00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:39.680
This doctor is saying
that Emily Rose said this.

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:41.600
Well, Emily Rose is not
the one who is testifying,

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:44.180
and, in fact, Emily Rose is
also conveying the things

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:46.540
that her priest told her as well.

00:06:46.540 --> 00:06:49.630
All of these things would
normally seem like hearsay.

00:06:49.630 --> 00:06:51.170
They are out-of-court statements.

00:06:51.170 --> 00:06:54.180
That's absolutely true,
and we can't cross examine,

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:56.560
necessarily, these particular witnesses.

00:06:56.560 --> 00:06:58.980
However, I don't think
this is actually hearsay

00:06:58.980 --> 00:07:01.300
because hearsay has to be an
out-of-court statement used

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:03.130
for the truth of the matter asserted.

00:07:03.130 --> 00:07:04.210
The truth of the matter asserted

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:08.710
is that Emily Rose was
feeling some sort of spiritual

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:10.050
encounter.

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:12.450
Whether or not that is
true is not the point

00:07:12.450 --> 00:07:13.850
of the statement being used.

00:07:13.850 --> 00:07:15.420
The point of the statement is to explain

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:18.570
why Emily did not go back to this doctor

00:07:18.570 --> 00:07:20.250
and receive more medical treatment.

00:07:20.250 --> 00:07:21.250
And the point of the statement

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:23.290
is to show why Emily Rose went

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:26.640
to her priest instead of
going back to a doctor.

00:07:26.640 --> 00:07:27.960
So whether or not she

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:32.500
was actually having a spiritual
encounter is irrelevant.

00:07:32.500 --> 00:07:34.510
The point of the statement
is to explain the actions

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:37.170
of both the victim and the actions

00:07:37.170 --> 00:07:39.180
of the defendant in this case.

00:07:39.180 --> 00:07:40.750
So I think that, under
these circumstances,

00:07:40.750 --> 00:07:43.110
it actually doesn't meet the
legal definition of hearsay.

00:07:43.110 --> 00:07:44.630
- Did you ask Emily why she

00:07:44.630 --> 00:07:47.290
had stopped filling her
Gambutrol prescriptions?

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:48.450
- Yes.

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:49.970
She said that Father Moore

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:52.080
had suggested she stop taking the drug.

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:53.530
- And what, in your medical opinion,

00:07:53.530 --> 00:07:55.890
was the direct result of
Father Moore's suggestion?

00:07:55.890 --> 00:07:56.830
- I firmly believe

00:07:56.830 --> 00:07:58.435
that Father Moore's suggestion killed her.

00:07:58.435 --> 00:07:59.610
- Objection, your Honor.
- Stopping the treatment

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:00.980
- The witness is asserting
- led directly to her death!

00:08:00.980 --> 00:08:04.330
- outrageous conclusions based
on pure hearsay evidence.

00:08:04.330 --> 00:08:06.100
- Technically correct, but I'll allow it

00:08:06.100 --> 00:08:08.223
as part of his expert opinion testimony.

00:08:09.204 --> 00:08:10.990
- Oh, boy, that was the wrong objection.

00:08:10.990 --> 00:08:13.490
The problem is not an
expert relying on hearsay.

00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:15.190
Experts are allowed to rely on hearsay.

00:08:15.190 --> 00:08:16.730
Experts are allowed to rely

00:08:16.730 --> 00:08:19.410
on otherwise inadmissible testimony

00:08:19.410 --> 00:08:21.740
to form their expert conclusions.

00:08:21.740 --> 00:08:26.150
However, this witness probably
doesn't have the expertise

00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:29.140
to be able to testify
to the cause of death,

00:08:29.140 --> 00:08:31.860
and this witness certainly
doesn't have the expertise

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:34.870
to be able to talk about
the legal causation here,

00:08:34.870 --> 00:08:37.220
which is even separate, potentially,

00:08:37.220 --> 00:08:40.660
from the medical reasoning
for why Emily Rose died.

00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:43.350
So that was totally improper testimony.

00:08:43.350 --> 00:08:46.130
The defense lawyer was right to object,

00:08:46.130 --> 00:08:49.130
but the defense lawyer
made the wrong objection

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:51.660
and, as a result, was overruled.

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:54.630
- Mr. Thomas has contended
that Emily Rose suffered

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:56.750
from a serious medical condition

00:08:56.750 --> 00:09:00.430
that required medical
treatment, nothing more.

00:09:00.430 --> 00:09:04.130
He has asserted that any attempt
by my client to help Emily

00:09:04.130 --> 00:09:06.390
by anything other than medical means

00:09:06.390 --> 00:09:09.993
was not only inappropriate
but criminally negligent.

00:09:11.450 --> 00:09:14.060
- Well, yeah, generally helping people

00:09:14.060 --> 00:09:18.470
with health conditions involves
actually using medical means

00:09:18.470 --> 00:09:20.370
so that is a reasonable standard

00:09:20.370 --> 00:09:22.980
for a prosecution to put out there.

00:09:22.980 --> 00:09:26.870
- Emily Rose's condition, in
fact, was demonic possession.

00:09:26.870 --> 00:09:29.593
An exorcism was her only hope for a cure.

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:32.020
Some of you may find yourself unable

00:09:32.020 --> 00:09:35.010
to reconcile Emily's beliefs or those

00:09:35.010 --> 00:09:37.080
of the defendant with your own.

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:40.230
You may not believe demons exist.

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:42.380
You won't have to because you will see

00:09:42.380 --> 00:09:44.630
that after the utter
failure of the doctors

00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:48.530
to help Emily, Father Moore
simply tried to help Emily

00:09:48.530 --> 00:09:51.220
in a different way using an approach

00:09:51.220 --> 00:09:55.370
that he, Emily, and her
family firmly believed

00:09:55.370 --> 00:09:56.973
was her only chance for relief.

00:09:58.080 --> 00:10:01.430
- Yeah, I don't actually think a judge

00:10:01.430 --> 00:10:03.000
would let that come in.

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:06.560
Effectively, you could
argue that the mental state

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:10.550
of the defendant is relevant,
that he legitimately believed

00:10:10.550 --> 00:10:15.200
that the victim was in some
sort of demonic possession.

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:17.300
But the thing is, the court is not going

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:19.060
to recognize the existence of demons.

00:10:19.060 --> 00:10:20.560
- [Man] Indochino.

00:10:21.669 --> 00:10:22.560
- Huh.

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:24.510
In reality, I think what would happen

00:10:24.510 --> 00:10:27.300
is that the judge would call a sidebar,

00:10:27.300 --> 00:10:29.200
she would specifically tell the attorney

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:31.670
that she's not allowed
to make the argument

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:34.680
that the demons were
actually responsible here.

00:10:34.680 --> 00:10:37.350
And if the lawyer insisted on doing it,

00:10:37.350 --> 00:10:39.340
then they would get an
instruction at the end

00:10:39.340 --> 00:10:42.410
that says the jury absolutely
cannot make that a basis

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:44.130
for their particular decision.

00:10:44.130 --> 00:10:45.400
It's a weird situation.

00:10:45.400 --> 00:10:46.963
- Where did you receive your education?

00:10:46.963 --> 00:10:49.700
- At Yale and then at Cambridge.

00:10:49.700 --> 00:10:52.210
- And what is your
specific area of expertise?

00:10:52.210 --> 00:10:54.370
- I study the spiritual experiences

00:10:54.370 --> 00:10:58.050
of people in various cultures
and the physiological

00:10:58.050 --> 00:11:01.680
and psychological changes they undergo

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:03.580
during these experiences.

00:11:03.580 --> 00:11:05.740
- Would it be accurate to
say that you specialize

00:11:05.740 --> 00:11:08.100
in the scientific study of possession?

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:09.180
- Yes.

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:13.920
Possession is one term for a
basic human experience reported

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:18.320
by a great number of people
all around the world.

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:21.420
In my fieldwork, I've seen many people

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:24.450
who experienced a sense of being invaded

00:11:24.450 --> 00:11:27.590
by an entity from the supernatural realm.

00:11:27.590 --> 00:11:31.110
- Okay, so despite the qualifications

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:33.820
of this supposed expert,
there are many reasons

00:11:33.820 --> 00:11:35.570
why this testimony doesn't come in.

00:11:35.570 --> 00:11:38.330
Number one, I think it's
an improper expert opinion.

00:11:38.330 --> 00:11:41.240
There is certain scientific
rigor that is required

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:44.060
to be able to testify
as an expert in court.

00:11:44.060 --> 00:11:47.230
And number two, it's more
prejudicial than it is probative.

00:11:47.230 --> 00:11:49.610
It's more likely to cause confusion

00:11:49.610 --> 00:11:51.350
and unfair prejudice than it is

00:11:51.350 --> 00:11:53.340
to actually clarify anything here.

00:11:53.340 --> 00:11:56.290
This sounds like total nonsense to me.

00:11:56.290 --> 00:12:00.283
- I believe that Emily Rose
was a hypersensitive person

00:12:01.330 --> 00:12:02.860
with an unusual connection to

00:12:02.860 --> 00:12:07.350
what Carlos Castaneda
called the separate reality.

00:12:07.350 --> 00:12:11.123
Hypersensitives are born
different from everyone else.

00:12:12.070 --> 00:12:13.930
- Yeah, at this point, the prosecution

00:12:13.930 --> 00:12:16.490
should really be objecting to relevance.

00:12:16.490 --> 00:12:18.090
Even if the judge lets it in,

00:12:18.090 --> 00:12:20.210
you need to preserve these
kind of things for appeal

00:12:20.210 --> 00:12:22.940
because this is a total nonsense argument.

00:12:22.940 --> 00:12:25.540
- They can have visions of the future

00:12:25.540 --> 00:12:30.350
or see the dead and sometimes
be uniquely susceptible

00:12:30.350 --> 00:12:33.280
to invasion by an entity
that is alien to them.

00:12:33.280 --> 00:12:34.153
- Objection.

00:12:35.330 --> 00:12:36.700
- Finally, geez.

00:12:36.700 --> 00:12:37.602
- [Judge] On what grounds?

00:12:37.602 --> 00:12:38.435
(hands slamming on desk)

00:12:38.435 --> 00:12:39.750
- How about silliness, your Honor?

00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:42.320
- No, silliness is not a valid objection.

00:12:42.320 --> 00:12:45.410
Object to relevance or
object to improper opinion.

00:12:45.410 --> 00:12:47.970
Silliness is not in the
Federal Rules of Evidence.

00:12:47.970 --> 00:12:49.520
- How do you know him?

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:52.000
- Well, I've known him
ever since parishioner

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:55.660
at St. Vincent's Church
about 15 years ago.

00:12:55.660 --> 00:12:57.470
I hadn't seen or heard of him for years

00:12:57.470 --> 00:12:58.933
when he called me last fall.

00:13:00.370 --> 00:13:02.330
He needed help, so.

00:13:02.330 --> 00:13:03.730
- What kind of help, Doctor?

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:07.080
- He wanted a medical perspective

00:13:07.080 --> 00:13:12.080
from a psychiatrist, one
that he knew and trusted,

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:15.320
to observe Emily's
physical and mental state.

00:13:15.320 --> 00:13:19.240
- So this would be
incredibly important evidence

00:13:19.240 --> 00:13:20.350
to put forward to say

00:13:20.350 --> 00:13:23.890
that the priest called
a medical professional

00:13:23.890 --> 00:13:26.400
to supervise everything to make sure that,

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:27.640
from a medical perspective,

00:13:27.640 --> 00:13:30.940
that the exorcism didn't
endanger the patient

00:13:30.940 --> 00:13:34.610
and that she was taken
care of during this time.

00:13:34.610 --> 00:13:36.540
Being able to say that you called a doctor

00:13:36.540 --> 00:13:37.590
in to supervise everything

00:13:37.590 --> 00:13:39.610
would probably be the most
important evidence you

00:13:39.610 --> 00:13:42.920
could possibly put on in
support of the defendant here.

00:13:42.920 --> 00:13:44.510
You would not wanna wait for that.

00:13:44.510 --> 00:13:47.630
- Before he was arrested,
Father Moore sent this to me,

00:13:47.630 --> 00:13:49.564
asked me to take care of it.

00:13:49.564 --> 00:13:50.570
- Oh!

00:13:50.570 --> 00:13:53.680
Now remember, audio
recordings can be hearsay.

00:13:53.680 --> 00:13:55.730
If you are recording another person,

00:13:55.730 --> 00:13:58.070
you are probably recording hearsay,

00:13:58.070 --> 00:14:00.660
and the tape itself is
also considered hearsay.

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:03.260
It's a common misconception
that things like videotapes

00:14:03.260 --> 00:14:06.260
or audiotapes or even
written documents themselves

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:09.740
are just, per se, admissible,
but on the contrary.

00:14:09.740 --> 00:14:12.570
Most of the time, those
things are considered hearsay,

00:14:12.570 --> 00:14:15.240
and you have to have a specific exception

00:14:15.240 --> 00:14:17.750
to the hearsay rule in order
to get those things in.

00:14:17.750 --> 00:14:19.410
But you also have a chain of custody

00:14:19.410 --> 00:14:21.220
and authentication issue here to make sure

00:14:21.220 --> 00:14:23.750
that this tape has not been tampered with

00:14:23.750 --> 00:14:25.160
and it is what it purports to be.

00:14:25.160 --> 00:14:27.840
So you could have a really
hard time bringing in things

00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:29.870
like audiotapes into court sometimes.

00:14:29.870 --> 00:14:30.970
- [Erin] Father Moore, before you

00:14:30.970 --> 00:14:32.660
could actually perform the exorcism,

00:14:32.660 --> 00:14:35.110
you had to get the sanction
of the church, correct?

00:14:35.110 --> 00:14:37.740
- Yes, on October the 27th last year,

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:39.930
I presented my assessment
and recommendations

00:14:39.930 --> 00:14:43.500
to the archbishop, and he
authorized the exorcism

00:14:43.500 --> 00:14:45.610
that day before I left the office.

00:14:45.610 --> 00:14:50.300
- So contrary to what you
see in movies and on TV,

00:14:50.300 --> 00:14:52.470
having the defendant testify in court

00:14:52.470 --> 00:14:55.650
is actually very, very
rare, mainly because people

00:14:55.650 --> 00:14:57.970
are really afraid of what

00:14:57.970 --> 00:14:59.640
might come out on cross examination.

00:14:59.640 --> 00:15:02.330
And frankly, the testimony of the accused

00:15:02.330 --> 00:15:05.191
is probably not going to
be particularly helpful.

00:15:05.191 --> 00:15:10.191
(dark terrifying music)
(rain pattering)

00:15:14.539 --> 00:15:15.680
- [Ethan] What do you
make of this gesture,

00:15:15.680 --> 00:15:17.780
this nod in your direction?

00:15:17.780 --> 00:15:18.613
- What?

00:15:19.760 --> 00:15:21.090
- I don't know.
(scoffs)

00:15:21.090 --> 00:15:22.480
Some kind of

00:15:22.480 --> 00:15:25.470
- Well that's not right.
- acknowledgement, I guess,

00:15:25.470 --> 00:15:27.098
that the game is on.

00:15:27.098 --> 00:15:29.870
- (laughs) It is totally
improper for the prosecutor

00:15:29.870 --> 00:15:31.930
to be questioning the defendant right now.

00:15:31.930 --> 00:15:33.390
Assuming we saw everything that we

00:15:33.390 --> 00:15:34.870
were gonna see from the defense,

00:15:34.870 --> 00:15:36.820
it's crazy that they didn't actually ask

00:15:36.820 --> 00:15:39.090
about the exorcism itself.

00:15:39.090 --> 00:15:42.570
You are sometimes allowed
to ask new questions

00:15:42.570 --> 00:15:45.090
of your witness; it's called redirect,

00:15:45.090 --> 00:15:47.450
which happens after cross examination.

00:15:47.450 --> 00:15:50.010
But the redirect is only limited to

00:15:50.010 --> 00:15:52.280
what is brought up in cross examination.

00:15:52.280 --> 00:15:54.380
And once you redirect, then, again,

00:15:54.380 --> 00:15:57.010
the prosecutor can
recross, but that recross

00:15:57.010 --> 00:15:59.690
is limited to whatever was
brought up during the redirect.

00:15:59.690 --> 00:16:03.550
So it's crazy malpractice
that they put this guy

00:16:03.550 --> 00:16:07.390
on the stand, made him subject
to whatever cross examination

00:16:07.390 --> 00:16:09.230
is happening here, but they didn't ask

00:16:09.230 --> 00:16:13.060
about the crucial thing that
they wanted to talk about here.

00:16:13.060 --> 00:16:15.870
- So this night, when you began
to prepare Emily's exorcism,

00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:18.490
was it your first encounter
with this demon ghost?

00:16:18.490 --> 00:16:19.323
- Yes.

00:16:19.323 --> 00:16:20.470
- Have you seen it again?

00:16:24.090 --> 00:16:25.688
- I've seen it ever since.

00:16:25.688 --> 00:16:28.605
(people murmuring)

00:16:31.080 --> 00:16:32.550
- [Ethan] No further
questions at this time.

00:16:32.550 --> 00:16:34.400
- There are so many more questions

00:16:34.400 --> 00:16:36.890
that you would ask at cross examination.

00:16:36.890 --> 00:16:38.040
- Redirect, your Honor.

00:16:39.333 --> 00:16:40.900
(scoffs)

00:16:40.900 --> 00:16:42.110
Father Moore,
- Okay.

00:16:42.110 --> 00:16:43.960
- after you received the
bishop's authorization,

00:16:43.960 --> 00:16:45.770
when did you proceed with an exorcism?

00:16:45.770 --> 00:16:47.630
- October 31st.

00:16:47.630 --> 00:16:48.880
- On Halloween.

00:16:48.880 --> 00:16:50.878
Is that a bit dramatic?

00:16:50.878 --> 00:16:52.250
- The Halloween tradition is based

00:16:52.250 --> 00:16:55.560
on legends throughout history
that indicate a real increase

00:16:55.560 --> 00:16:57.047
in spiritual activity on that night,

00:16:57.047 --> 00:16:58.980
and I thought we could use the occasion

00:16:58.980 --> 00:17:00.433
to draw them into the open.

00:17:01.350 --> 00:17:04.360
- Yeah, okay, so this is
totally improper for redirect

00:17:04.360 --> 00:17:06.730
because these are questions
that you would ask

00:17:06.730 --> 00:17:08.730
on the original direct examination.

00:17:08.730 --> 00:17:10.940
So you don't get a
second bite at the apple

00:17:10.940 --> 00:17:13.350
to go into redirect into these subjects

00:17:13.350 --> 00:17:15.630
that you didn't bring out the first time.

00:17:15.630 --> 00:17:18.030
- I offer into evidence
this cassette recorder

00:17:18.030 --> 00:17:18.863
and audiotape

00:17:18.863 --> 00:17:19.696
- this defense
- Objection,

00:17:19.696 --> 00:17:20.529
- if it's A and B.
- Your Honor.

00:17:20.529 --> 00:17:22.130
People were not made aware
of the existence of this tape

00:17:22.130 --> 00:17:23.900
until late last night.
- Neither were we, your Honor.

00:17:23.900 --> 00:17:25.640
This tape is new evidence that just came

00:17:25.640 --> 00:17:27.390
into our possession yesterday evening.

00:17:27.390 --> 00:17:29.040
We have provided counsel for the people

00:17:29.040 --> 00:17:30.750
with the transcript of its contents.

00:17:30.750 --> 00:17:33.260
- You mean this tape was not
given to you by your client?

00:17:33.260 --> 00:17:34.270
- No, your Honor.

00:17:34.270 --> 00:17:36.210
We received it from a reluctant witness,

00:17:36.210 --> 00:17:38.010
Dr. Graham Cartwright,
who will also be called

00:17:38.010 --> 00:17:39.610
to testify to its authenticity.

00:17:39.610 --> 00:17:40.443
- Your Honor--

00:17:40.443 --> 00:17:41.440
- The people will find his name

00:17:41.440 --> 00:17:43.410
on the updated witness list we provided

00:17:43.410 --> 00:17:44.580
to them this morning.

00:17:44.580 --> 00:17:47.480
- Yeah, okay, so there are
a couple big problems here.

00:17:47.480 --> 00:17:49.990
Judges hate being
blindsided, and, presumably,

00:17:49.990 --> 00:17:51.600
there have been months since the start

00:17:51.600 --> 00:17:53.400
of this particular trial.

00:17:53.400 --> 00:17:57.610
The odds of this evidence being
authentic are pretty slim,

00:17:57.610 --> 00:18:01.510
and to not call the person
who provided the audiotape

00:18:01.510 --> 00:18:03.200
and when it's not the defendant,

00:18:03.200 --> 00:18:05.120
those are just huge red flags

00:18:05.120 --> 00:18:08.610
that this is inauthentic,
it's something that's fake,

00:18:08.610 --> 00:18:11.220
it didn't come from the
defendant who allegedly made it.

00:18:11.220 --> 00:18:13.200
There are chain of custody problems.

00:18:13.200 --> 00:18:16.780
It's being sprung on the
prosecution in the middle of trial.

00:18:16.780 --> 00:18:18.300
I think all of this means that the judge

00:18:18.300 --> 00:18:21.341
would probably not allow
this audiotape to come in.

00:18:21.341 --> 00:18:22.174
(dramatic music)

00:18:22.174 --> 00:18:24.830
- [Judge] Is the defense ready to proceed?

00:18:24.830 --> 00:18:26.210
- Yes, your Honor.

00:18:26.210 --> 00:18:28.978
The defense recalls Father Richard Moore.

00:18:28.978 --> 00:18:29.930
- Ugh.

00:18:29.930 --> 00:18:32.410
You don't get to just recall a witness

00:18:32.410 --> 00:18:33.700
whenever you feel like it.

00:18:33.700 --> 00:18:35.950
This defense attorney is getting
three bites of the apple.

00:18:35.950 --> 00:18:37.360
They called the defendant once.

00:18:37.360 --> 00:18:39.020
Then they didn't ask all the questions

00:18:39.020 --> 00:18:39.853
that they should have.

00:18:39.853 --> 00:18:41.760
And then they called the defendant again

00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:45.210
to do a redirect examination,
which went into topics

00:18:45.210 --> 00:18:47.260
that were not proper at all to talk about

00:18:47.260 --> 00:18:48.530
because they didn't bring
'em up the first time.

00:18:48.530 --> 00:18:50.610
Then that witness was excused,

00:18:50.610 --> 00:18:52.190
and they brought the witness back again

00:18:52.190 --> 00:18:54.410
to testify for a third time.

00:18:54.410 --> 00:18:56.390
This is the kind of thing
that judges refer to

00:18:56.390 --> 00:18:59.530
as a circus, and all of
this would be improper.

00:18:59.530 --> 00:19:01.450
- When did you last see Emily alive?

00:19:01.450 --> 00:19:02.830
- The night before she died.

00:19:02.830 --> 00:19:04.760
Her mother, Maria, called me and said

00:19:04.760 --> 00:19:06.380
that Emily had asked for me.

00:19:06.380 --> 00:19:08.070
- Why did she ask to see you?

00:19:08.070 --> 00:19:10.560
- Why didn't you ask him about
this the first time around?

00:19:10.560 --> 00:19:11.950
Ugh, man.

00:19:11.950 --> 00:19:13.410
- To give me this.

00:19:13.410 --> 00:19:16.160
- Did Emily explain to you
the letter's significance?

00:19:16.160 --> 00:19:18.550
- Yes, she said she'd
written it the morning

00:19:18.550 --> 00:19:21.830
after the failed exorcism and that it

00:19:21.830 --> 00:19:24.060
was important for me to share.

00:19:24.060 --> 00:19:24.893
- Boo.

00:19:24.893 --> 00:19:27.700
So apparently, the victim wrote a letter

00:19:27.700 --> 00:19:30.180
to the defendant before she died.

00:19:30.180 --> 00:19:32.870
This feels like there's some
major hearsay problems here,

00:19:32.870 --> 00:19:33.892
but let me think about this.

00:19:33.892 --> 00:19:36.800
(upbeat rock music)

00:19:36.800 --> 00:19:40.180
I guess, conceivably, it doesn't
really matter necessarily

00:19:40.180 --> 00:19:42.610
about the truth of what's in the letter,

00:19:42.610 --> 00:19:45.180
but I guess it can go to the state of mind

00:19:45.180 --> 00:19:48.780
of the priest in terms of
the actions that he took

00:19:48.780 --> 00:19:50.090
or the actions that he didn't take.

00:19:50.090 --> 00:19:51.910
Yeah, arguably, you're not using the truth

00:19:51.910 --> 00:19:53.640
in the matter asserted,
but you're using it

00:19:53.640 --> 00:19:55.430
for the effect on the
listener and the effect

00:19:55.430 --> 00:19:59.660
on the mental state of
the victim in this case.

00:19:59.660 --> 00:20:01.420
I think maybe that letter does come in

00:20:01.420 --> 00:20:03.670
as non hearsay 'cause it's not being used

00:20:03.670 --> 00:20:05.493
for the truth of the matter asserted.

00:20:07.850 --> 00:20:10.080
- [Judge] Please read
the verdict out loud.

00:20:10.080 --> 00:20:11.500
- In my experience, most times,

00:20:11.500 --> 00:20:14.810
the judges don't make the
foreman actually read the verdict

00:20:14.810 --> 00:20:17.360
because that's a lot of
pressure to put on one person.

00:20:17.360 --> 00:20:21.120
So usually, the judge reads
the verdict of the jury,

00:20:21.120 --> 00:20:24.180
and they don't put the poor foreperson

00:20:24.180 --> 00:20:26.470
on the spotlight like
this, but it's dramatic.

00:20:26.470 --> 00:20:28.870
So it's not surprising
you see it like this.

00:20:28.870 --> 00:20:32.090
- In the case of the people
versus Father Richard Moore,

00:20:32.090 --> 00:20:37.090
we the jury find the defendant
guilty of negligent homicide.

00:20:37.660 --> 00:20:38.493
- Makes sense.

00:20:38.493 --> 00:20:41.093
- The court thank the
jury for its service.

00:20:42.060 --> 00:20:46.640
Sentencing for this case
will be set for April 3rd.

00:20:46.640 --> 00:20:47.560
- That's also good.

00:20:47.560 --> 00:20:49.710
You don't see that often in movies.

00:20:49.710 --> 00:20:52.280
Usually, the sentencing is right away,

00:20:52.280 --> 00:20:55.890
but sentencing is almost
always at a later time

00:20:55.890 --> 00:20:57.530
after some briefing to make sure

00:20:57.530 --> 00:20:58.630
that they get the sentencing right

00:20:58.630 --> 00:21:01.160
because sentencing is
totally separate from

00:21:01.160 --> 00:21:05.220
whether they establish guilt
or not, generally speaking.

00:21:05.220 --> 00:21:07.510
- Your Honor, if it pleases the court,

00:21:07.510 --> 00:21:09.810
Father Moore requested at
the start of trial that,

00:21:09.810 --> 00:21:12.440
if found guilty, he would
be sentenced immediately.

00:21:12.440 --> 00:21:13.273
- So what?

00:21:13.273 --> 00:21:14.860
It doesn't matter what he requests.

00:21:14.860 --> 00:21:16.690
- [Judge] Does the
prosecution have any objection

00:21:16.690 --> 00:21:18.980
to expedited sentencing?

00:21:18.980 --> 00:21:20.490
- They should.

00:21:20.490 --> 00:21:21.830
- Very well.
(laughs)

00:21:21.830 --> 00:21:23.440
- Sentencing is a complicated thing,

00:21:23.440 --> 00:21:27.100
and many states and the federal system

00:21:27.100 --> 00:21:30.300
have complicated calculations
you have to go through

00:21:30.300 --> 00:21:33.127
to make sure that the
sentence fits the crime.

00:21:33.127 --> 00:21:37.110
And you don't want wild
discrepancies between people

00:21:37.110 --> 00:21:39.420
who have committed the
exactly the same crime.

00:21:39.420 --> 00:21:41.210
- Are there any statements
you would like to make

00:21:41.210 --> 00:21:43.460
before I impose this sentence?

00:21:43.460 --> 00:21:44.780
- No, your Honor.

00:21:44.780 --> 00:21:46.680
- Very well.
- Excuse me, your Honor,

00:21:47.730 --> 00:21:50.040
but the jury would like
to make a recommendation

00:21:50.040 --> 00:21:51.220
regarding the sentence.
- Objection.

00:21:51.220 --> 00:21:52.640
- That's totally improper.

00:21:52.640 --> 00:21:54.300
- Sit down, Counselor.

00:21:54.300 --> 00:21:57.920
It's only a recommendation,
and I would like to hear it.

00:21:57.920 --> 00:21:58.913
- Ugh, still.

00:22:00.370 --> 00:22:02.693
- We recommend a sentence of time served.

00:22:03.617 --> 00:22:06.534
(people murmuring)

00:22:11.000 --> 00:22:12.980
- Yeah, that is not correct.

00:22:12.980 --> 00:22:14.750
- I'll accept that.
(laughs)

00:22:14.750 --> 00:22:18.620
(bright jaunty music)

00:22:18.620 --> 00:22:21.260
- All right, that is The
Exorcism of Emily Rose,

00:22:21.260 --> 00:22:24.035
and now it's time to give it
a grade for legal realism.

00:22:24.035 --> 00:22:26.843
(gavel banging)

00:22:26.843 --> 00:22:28.330
One the one hand, you have some aspects

00:22:28.330 --> 00:22:30.500
of the trial that were totally spot on.

00:22:30.500 --> 00:22:32.410
The prosecutor was using the lectern,

00:22:32.410 --> 00:22:34.330
they were making pretty
reasonable arguments.

00:22:34.330 --> 00:22:36.890
There weren't a whole lot
of histrionics going on.

00:22:36.890 --> 00:22:38.850
People weren't yelling and shouting

00:22:38.850 --> 00:22:40.900
and saying you can't handle the truth.

00:22:40.900 --> 00:22:44.010
So the way that this homicide trial

00:22:44.010 --> 00:22:46.650
was handled was actually
very, very realistic.

00:22:46.650 --> 00:22:49.590
On the other hand, you have
the admission of a whole bunch

00:22:49.590 --> 00:22:53.550
of totally improper evidence,
improper expert opinions,

00:22:53.550 --> 00:22:55.770
and they completely
mangled the way that you

00:22:55.770 --> 00:22:59.180
would actually have the
examination of a defendant

00:22:59.180 --> 00:23:02.460
being recalled three or
four times in the trial.

